<Page
  article
  title="Instagram export troubleshooting: missing files and incomplete lists"
  description="Resolve missing followers or following files, split JSON exports, unsupported formats, source-date uncertainty, and browser memory safeguards."
>
  <p class="eyebrow">GUIDE · LOCAL IMPORT TROUBLESHOOTING</p><h1>
    When an export looks incomplete.
  </h1><p class="lede">
    A convincing list can still be missing data. Start with the source and the
    error message before drawing conclusions about a relationship.
  </p>
  <h2>“A required direction is missing”</h2><p>
    A follower count is not a followers list. The checker needs supported
    records for both followers and following. Select the full ZIP or the
    relevant JSON files for both directions. A missing file is different from a
    valid, explicitly empty list, and MutualLens must not silently turn one into
    the other.
  </p>
  <h2>“Not following you back” is withheld</h2><p>
    Without complete, compatible inputs, absence cannot be classified safely.
    Confirm that you selected all the parts from both directions and that they
    belong to the same account and export. The all-parts checkbox defaults off.
    Some detected problems, such as missing numbered parts or ambiguous
    identities, can still prevent a complete result after confirmation.
  </p>
  <h2>Followers are spread across several files</h2><p>
    Keep every numbered part. A <code>followers_1.json</code> file does not guarantee
    it is the only follower file. Selecting multiple relevant JSON files lets the
    parser combine their records. Mixing files from separate exports can introduce
    duplicates, date mismatches, or identity problems; use one consistent source.
  </p>
  <h2>The file is HTML or an unknown JSON schema</h2><p>
    The current preview supports tested JSON export shapes. Renaming a file does
    not change its format. Request a suitable JSON export through Meta’s
    official controls where available, or preserve the error details for a
    parser-format report. Never share real graph files in a public issue.
  </p>
  <h2>The file exceeds a memory safeguard</h2><p>
    The preview’s local input budget is 64 MiB across at most 2,000 selected
    files. The parser separately enforces expanded archive, per-file, path,
    entry, and compression-ratio safeguards. These limits protect browser
    resources; they do not truncate a dataset at a follower count.
  </p><p>
    A full account archive may contain unnecessary media and messages. Select
    only the relevant followers and following JSON parts from your own export.
    If those still exceed the supported resource budget, the preview cannot
    safely process them on this path.
  </p>
  <h2>Snapshot comparison rejects dates or identity</h2><p>
    The same save date does not mean the same collection date. Both sources need
    known collection dates and must describe the same account. Where only a
    username is available, the comparison carries rename and reactivation
    uncertainty. Keep an unknown source date blank instead of guessing.
  </p>
  <h2>The totals differ from Instagram’s profile</h2><p>
    A profile counter and a source collected at another time may differ.
    Duplicates, account changes, or source omissions can also matter. Do not
    adjust the data until a total looks right. A matching total is supporting
    context, not proof that every account was retrieved.
  </p>
